WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:06.860
We will start analyzing spectra by first
looking at an unrealistic ideal spectra,

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:09.970
and then we'll move to real spectra.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:13.860
Let's start from analyzing
this short peptide.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:19.790
Let's generate all its prefix peptides and
all its suffix peptide.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:26.220
Let's measure the masses of all prefix
peptides and all suffix peptides, and

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:32.010
our goal is, from these masses, to
reconstruct the original peptide.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:32.680
Can we do this?

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:38.980
Well, an important thing to realize
is that we don't know which masses

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.590
correspond to prefix fragments and which
masses correspond to suffix fragments, and

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:48.400
therefore, what we are given is
the ideal spectrum of the peptide,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.770
which is simply a collection of
both prefix and suffix masses.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:01:00.320
We will say that a given
peptide "explains" a spectrum

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.580
if the ideal spectrum of this
peptide is equal to the spectrum.

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:12.720
So we have now a decoding problem,
which is to reconstruct

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.870
a peptide from its ideal spectrum.
Input: A collection of integers, Spectrum.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:24.090
Output: An amino acid string
Peptide that explains Spectrum.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.590
To solve it, let's construct a graph.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:33.690
We will form a set of nodes of this
graph by designing a single node for

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:36.270
every element of the spectrum,

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:42.790
and we will construct edges of the graph
by connecting two nodes if the difference

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:48.340
between their corresponding labels
is equal to the mass of amino acids,

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:52.770
and we will label this edge by
the mass of this amino acid.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.360
Here's the graph we construct for
our ideal spectrum, and

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:01.340
what will be the solution of our problem
of decoding an ideal spectrum problem?

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:08.190
Of course, we need to walk from
the source node 0 to the sink, and

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:14.600
the path that we'll spell will correspond
to the peptide we are trying to find.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:22.450
As a result, I propose the following
Decoding Ideal Spectrum Algorithm:

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:26.070
First, construct a graph from Spectrum.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:31.810
Second, find a path from source
to sink in the resulting graph.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:36.830
And finally, return the amino acid
string spelled by this path.

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.780
That sounds simple, but
does it really work for all spectra?

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:46.940
It clearly worked for the
simple spectrum that we considered before.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:49.110
Let's take a look at this spectrum,

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:52.540
and I will tell you that this is an ideal
spectrum of an unknown peptide.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:03:00.840
Let's construct the graph of Spectrum and
let's consider this path in this graph.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:05.150
This graph spells out the peptide NTTAG.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:09.150
But if we take the ideal
spectrum of NTTAG,

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:14.940
it will turn out that it is not
equal to the original spectrum,

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.390
which means that we
are doing something wrong.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:22.847
Of course, in this graph, there is
a path that spells the correct

00:03:22.847 --> 00:03:27.063
solution of the problem, but
we don't know how to find it.

00:03:27.063 --> 00:03:32.486
What we can do, however, is to consider
all possible paths in the graph, and

00:03:32.486 --> 00:03:37.488
this is done by a new version of
the Decoding Ideal Spectrum Algorithm,

00:03:37.488 --> 00:03:43.617
that first constructs the graph of Spectrum,
explores all paths in the resulting graph,

00:03:43.617 --> 00:03:47.359
but for each path,
it first checks the condition,

00:03:47.359 --> 00:03:52.331
if the ideal spectrum of the peptide
is really equal to the spectrum.

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:56.710
If this condition is met,
then it returns the peptide.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.580
So this is definitely a correct
solution of the problem,

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.770
but is it an efficient solution?

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:07.190
You will see it that it's actually
an exponential algorithm, but

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:13.040
there are polynomial solutions for this problem
that we will not cover in this course.

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.060
Our goal, however,

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:21.490
is to move from decoding ideal
spectra to the decoding real spectra.

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:26.030
And on this line you see an ideal
spectrum will be some false and

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:29.380
missing masses and the real spectrum.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:34.730
And the reality is that, in spectra generated
by mass spectrometers, there are usually

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:40.340
many false and missing masses,
thus complicating our problem of

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:46.330
decoding a real spectrum,
that we will consider in the next lecture.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:51.060
An important thing about decoding a real
spectrum is that its objective is to

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:56.150
find an amino acid string Peptide
that explains Spectrum the best.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:02.505
But what does it mean to be the "best"
peptide explaining the spectrum?

00:05:02.505 --> 00:05:04.170
We will learn about this
in the next segment.

